      <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-muted-foreground mb-1">Direct Answer</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">Shoulders are the third most affected body region for office workers with musculoskeletal disorders, affecting 37.4% of cases (<a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-30155-6" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Scientific Reports, 2025</a>). For users above 6'1", the root cause is usually the same: standard chair armrests are designed for the 95th-percentile male at ~185 cm and sit too low to support the forearms of taller users, forcing the trapezius and deltoid to fire continuously to hold arm weight throughout the workday.</p>
